<BaseLayout
  title="How to Apply for a Dudley Private Hire Driver Licence"
  description="How to apply for a Dudley private hire driver licence: over 18, two years driving, enhanced DBS, Group 2 medical, 20-question law test, driving assessment."
>
  <main class="md:flex md:justify-center">
    <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-[750px]">
      <h1>How to Apply for a Dudley Private Hire Driver Licence</h1>

      <p>
        <strong>Last checked: October 2026.</strong> Requirements and fees can
        change. Always check
        <a href="https://www.dudley.gov.uk/business/licences-registrations-and-permits/taxi-and-private-hire-vehicles" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          >Dudley Metropolitan Borough Council's official taxi and private hire pages</a
        >
        before you apply or pay anything.
      </p>

      <h2>Eligibility</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You must be <strong>over 18 years old</strong>.</li>
        <li>You must hold a full UK driving licence that authorises you to drive a motor vehicle, and have held it for <strong>at least two years</strong>.</li>
        <li>You must have the right to work in the UK. If you are not a UK citizen, you must also provide a <strong>certificate of good conduct</strong>, the equivalent of a DBS check, issued by an agency in the country of your citizenship.</li>
        <li>You must pass an <strong>enhanced DBS</strong> check and meet the DVLA <strong>Group 2</strong> medical standard.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>How to apply</h2>
      <p>Dudley works by appointment. You complete the forms, then book a time to bring everything to the licensing office. <strong>Payment is taken once your application has been checked.</strong></p>
      <h3>Step 1: Get the application form</h3>
      <p>Download the <strong>Private Hire Driver's Application Form</strong> from the Dudley Council website, or get one from the licensing office.</p>
      <h3>Step 2: Apply for your enhanced DBS</h3>
      <p>Apply online through <strong>Dudley Online Disclosures</strong>. When it asks, enter the Organisation Reference <strong>EXTERNALTAXI</strong>. <strong>No password is needed.</strong> Your login details at this stage are case sensitive.</p>
      <p>Once you have completed the online application, make an appointment with an <strong>ID Checker in the Licensing Team</strong>, who checks your identity as part of the DBS application. All drivers must have a new DBS check <strong>every three years</strong>.</p>
      <h3>Step 3: Get your DVLA share code</h3>
      <p>Generate your DVLA "share your licence" code, so the licensing section can check your driving licence history.</p>
      <h3>Step 4: Medical</h3>
      <p>You need a <strong>Group 2 medical</strong> with your GP. Group 2 is the national standard for lorry and bus drivers. Dudley says details of the medical process are provided when you apply.</p>
      <p>How often it is repeated depends on your age:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Your first licence:</strong> a medical is required.</li>
        <li><strong>Aged 19 to 45:</strong> no repeat medicals, unless your medical condition changes (you must then tell both the DVLA and the Licensing Authority).</li>
        <li><strong>At 45:</strong> a further medical, then <strong>every three years up to 65</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>From 65:</strong> a medical <strong>every year</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If the doctor raises any concerns, the matter is referred to the Head of Service, who decides how often you need one.</p>
      <h3>Step 5: Book your appointment and bring your documents</h3>
      <p>Contact the licensing office to book a time. Bring:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Your completed application form</li>
        <li><strong>Two passport photographs</strong> and a form of photo ID</li>
        <li>Your full UK driving licence and your DVLA share code</li>
        <li>The code that lets the licensing section apply for your enhanced DBS certificate</li>
        <li>Your GP's details, so a satisfactory medical certificate can be obtained</li>
        <li>If you are not a UK citizen: your certificate of good conduct, and evidence that you are eligible to work in the UK</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Step 6: Law test</h3>
      <p>New private hire applicants take a <strong>law test</strong>. It is <strong>20 multiple choice questions in 30 minutes, and you must answer at least 16 correctly</strong>. The questions are about the law and the conditions of a private hire driver's licence and a private hire vehicle licence.</p>
      <p><strong>There is no local knowledge test for private hire.</strong> From April 2025, new private hire applicants no longer have to take it. It is still required for hackney carriage drivers. Dudley provides a pack with further details when you apply.</p>
      <h3>Step 7: Driving assessment</h3>
      <p>You must pass a <strong>driving assessment</strong> with one of Dudley Council's approved suppliers. <strong>You pay for it yourself.</strong></p>
      <h2>Once you are licensed</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Convictions:</strong> tell the licensing office in writing <strong>within 7 days</strong>. This covers court convictions, fixed penalty endorsements, police cautions, and other fixed penalties.</li>
        <li><strong>Change of address:</strong> tell the council <strong>forthwith</strong>, which means immediately and without delay.</li>
        <li><strong>A medical condition you are diagnosed with:</strong> tell the council <strong>forthwith</strong>, in writing.</li>
        <li><strong>DBS:</strong> a new check every three years.</li>
        <li><strong>Unclaimed lost property:</strong> take it to the <strong>Council's Licensing Office</strong> within 48 hours, and get a receipt.</li>
        <li><strong>Your vehicle:</strong> it must be licensed with Dudley and pass the council's compliance inspection. Vehicles are tested every 12 months while they are under six years old, every 6 months from six to under ten years, and every 4 months from ten years. Vehicle tests are booked through the licensing office.</li>
      </ul>
